--TEST--
`Core\Cache::local` and `Core\Cache::shared` are two members with two contracts rather than one API with a flag, and the tier a program chose is visible where it wrote it — ADR 0059 § 1
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 1: the tier is the member's own name. Neither takes an argument, so there
// is no value computed at run time that could turn one call into the other —
// which is what makes the choice reviewable rather than merely documented.
var $cache = Core\Cache::local();
$cache->put("greeting", "hello");
var $back = $cache->get("greeting") as string;
echo "local=", $back, "\n";

// § 1: the coherent tier is a different member making a different promise, and
// a deployment that has configured no store is told so by name. It is never
// handed the per-core store under the other tier's name, because a program that
// asked for coherence and got forgetfulness would be wrong in a way nothing
// downstream could detect.
try {
    Core\Cache::shared();
    echo "shared handed back a store\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
local=hello
Core\Cache::shared(): no shared store is configured, so there is nothing coherent to answer with — set `[cache.shared] url`, or use `Core\Cache::local()` and accept its contract
